WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.060
I choose you, Film Theory!

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:04.680
Use mind-blow!

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.140
We'll see how effective it is.

00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:08.860
Don't change that YouTube dial, ladies and gentlemen.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:10.540
Yes, you are on the right channel.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.220
Today, we're covering the upcoming Pokémon movie, "Detective Pikachu".

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:21.200
A film that made the ballsy choice of having video games' cutest mascot voiced by Deadpool.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:22.560
"Nobody's getting hurt!"

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.580
*crash*

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.740
And man, has it worked!

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:31.180
These trailers are setting the internet on fire with this bizarre cognitive dissonance that they're creating.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.100
Cognitive dissonance, just so you know,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:36.400
is when you have conflicting thoughts and attitudes existing at the same time.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.800
Like when you smoke, but you know that it's bad for you.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.800
Or here, where you think "Oh, what a cute little Pokémon!",

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.860
and then hear his creepy man voice
popping out of your favorite Pika!

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:46.000
"Get me the hell outta here!"

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.280
They've been the first trailers in a long time to actually make me feel optimistic about the prospect of a live-action video game movie,

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:57.740
even if all the Pokémon are eerily fuzzy, or look like they've just lost a ton of weight.

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.120
I mean, apparently Charizard has been getting himself on the Weight Watchers!

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.500
Look at those rolls!

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:06.460
Now, "Detective Pikachu" features an entirely new main character, Tim Goodman,

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:12.000
who explores Ryme city, a completely new location that we've never seen before in any other Pokémon media,

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.339
aside from the game of the same name, which to be clear, I will be ignoring for today's theory.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.000
Maybe I'll cover it later on Game Theory, or after the movie releases, and compare and contrast the two, who knows!

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.860
Tim's father goes missing,

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.829
and shortly thereafter, he stumbles across a Pikachu that can talk and considers itself to be a detective.

00:01:28.829 --> 00:01:30.829
"I don't need a Pokémon! Period!"

00:01:30.829 --> 00:01:32.200
"Then what about a world class detective?!"

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.680
Beyond that though, this talking Pikachu is a complete mystery,

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:38.340
considering he just woke up with amnesia.

00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:41.360
"Woke up with a heavy case of amnesia in the middle of nowhere."

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.360
Memory loss, you say?

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.000
You know what that means!

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.040
This 'mon has clearly got himself some history, which means a theory.

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:53.980
So, while the obvious answer is probably that Pikachu is somehow connected to Tim's missing father,

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:57.059
or, heck, IS Tim's missing father!

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:02.540
I had a more exciting idea pitched to me on Reddit by /u/Simple_Meme_Lord that I'd like to explore.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.909
That this Pikachu is Ash's Pikachu?

00:02:05.909 --> 00:02:07.760
Yes, Ash from the anime.

00:02:07.760 --> 00:02:09.040
As different as this movie seems,

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.860
is there a chance that this could be a part of the same canon timeline as the TV show?

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:20.980
Could the secret of Detective Pikachu's forgotten history actually be that he served as Ash's faithful companion,

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:23.979
for over a thousand episodes of animated splendor?

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.880
It sounds like an outlandish idea, and heck, it probably is,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.520
but the more you dig into the countless details that are hidden in these trailers,

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.220
the more and more all of this starts to make sense.

00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:37.000
Let's do this, Detective Pikachu style!

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:40.240
I choose you, corkboard of evidence!

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.159
Clue number one: Ryme City.

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:43.640
Now, as I already mentioned,

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:49.359
"Detective Pikachu" is set in a city that we've never seen before in the previous mainline Pokémon games or the TV show.

00:02:49.359 --> 00:02:54.519
However, it would be a huge mistake to assume that this means it takes place in a completely separate world.

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:59.828
You see, we get several clear indications in the trailer that, though Ryme City is in a new location,

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:04.300
it's set in the same Pokémon universe that we're already familiar with.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:08.480
In Tim's bedroom, we see that he has a poster for "Sinnoh Championship XXIV",

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:13.160
an obvious reference to the region that's introduced in the "Diamond &amp; Pearl" games and TV seasons.

00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:17.360
A more detailed look at his bedroom wall shows posters for the Johto sports club,

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.359
another reference to a region that we're already familiar with from the Johto League seasons of the anime.

00:03:22.359 --> 00:03:25.300
While Ash's journeys have never taken us to Ryme City before,

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:29.640
it's pretty safe to conclude that we're still in the same established Pokemon world.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:36.660
Still, it would be a huge leap to assume that just because we're seeing the names of familiar locations that we're also in the TV show.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:40.800
After all, the video games have the exact same locations and regions as the anime.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.900
But the games and anime follow a completely different canon.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:46.160
The video games don't have a character named Ash Ketchum,

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:49.540
who travels around Kanto, with Brock and Misty for instance.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:54.120
and even within the games themselves, there's already a confirmed multiverse.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:56.289
One with mega evolutions, and one without.

00:03:56.419 --> 00:04:00.369
For more information on that, I go way in depth in this episode of Game Theory.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:06.400
However, the trailer gives us another big hint about a link between the "Detective Pikachu" movie and the TV show,

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:09.660
leading us to clue number two: Jigglypuff.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.900
For one brief moment in the trailer, we see a Jigglypuff,

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.279
but not just any Jigglypuff.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.180
Look at what it has in its hand - a microphone with the green cover.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:21.400
Now, this could just be an easter egg,

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:26.100
but any fan of the anime will immediately recognize what's going on in that scene.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:30.100
First introduced in Episode 45 of the anime, the song of Jigglypuff,

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:36.800
this one particular Pokémon has been a recurring character on the TV show for over 20 years,

00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:38.040
showing up to sing a lullaby,

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:40.800
getting angry when the lullaby puts everyone to sleep,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:44.240
and then trolling everyone by pulling the cap off its microphone,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:48.000
to reveal that it was actually a marker the whole time!

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.380
Which it then uses to draw on everyone's faces like the only sober guy at a frat party.

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:58.080
Carrying around that iconic microphone isn't something that's inherently part of a Jigglypuff's DNA,

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.460
it is something that is specifically tied to one unique character in the anime,

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:06.720
a character who now seems to be appearing in the trailer for Detective Pikachu.

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:10.180
and if one Pokémon from the anime managed to find its way into Ryme City,

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.280
surely Ash's Pikachu could have done the same.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.120
Clue the third and fourth: Fragments of memory.

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.580
Now, let's focus a bit more on our main character - Detective Pikachu himself.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:25.989
We don't get too much information about him, on account of that amnesia, but we do learn one key fact:

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:29.720
"So you're a talking Pikachu with no memories who's addicted to caffeine."

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.720
He's addicted to caffeine, specifically coffee.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.380
This, according to the original creators of the game, was a riff on the trope of Detectives using pipes,

00:05:38.380 --> 00:05:43.100
a way to make Pikachu feel older and more hard-bitten, but still keep him family friendly.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.400
And while coffee itself doesn't appear in the anime,

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:52.360
from what I can find we do have plenty of scenes over the years of Ash's Pikachu eagerly drinking tea.

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:56.649
clearly he's a character who likes human beverages, especially beverages that are caffeinated.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:02.049
So making the jump from tea to coffee in an older, more adult film would make perfect sense.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.420
The only other thing that we know about him is that, despite having amnesia,

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.000
Pikachu remembers having dealt with Charizards in the past.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:12.840
"Now, obviously I've dealt with this putz before"

00:06:12.919 --> 00:06:16.089
Note that he says "dealt with", and not "battled".

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.560
You see, Ash's Pikachu never truly battles Charizard in the anime.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:26.260
But, both Ash and Pikachu had to deal with all sorts of misbehaving difficult Charizards in the past.

00:06:26.260 --> 00:06:31.060
So, this would actually line up very well with our theory that they're one and the same.

00:06:31.060 --> 00:06:33.840
It's a similar story here with Mr. Mime as well.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.308
In the trailer, Pikachu seems to have a solid understanding of how to deal with the awkward mime Pokémon.

00:06:39.309 --> 00:06:40.360
"He's barely moving!"

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.680
"Don't tell him that!"

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:45.080
Something that Ash's Pikachu would also be familiar with,

00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:50.180
considering his master's mother had a Mime constantly working in their household.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.940
And finally, clue the fifth: let's talk talking.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:07:00.340
It's easy to overlook, but obviously, the most striking thing about this character is that he can talk,

00:07:00.340 --> 00:07:03.820
except, it's not 100% true.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:09.820
the trailer makes it very clear that while Tim can understand what Pikachu is saying, no other human can.

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.640
"You can hear him, right?"

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:12.700
"Pika-pika!"

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:15.900
"Yeah, pika-pika-pika, he's adorable!

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.860
"You're adorable!"

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:17.660
"They can't understand me."

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.020
Afraid not there, Tim.

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:21.180
Pikachu isn't actually speaking English,

00:07:21.180 --> 00:07:25.400
he's capable of speaking in a way that's only understood by Tim.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:30.140
And that sounds an awful lot like how we see Pikachu communicating with Ash throughout the anime,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:33.280
even if he's not voiced by Ryan Reynolds.

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:34.360
"Pikachu!"

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.200
"You wanna go too, buddy?

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.200
"Pika!"

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:37.840
"Right!"

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:41.160
And we don't just have to infer it off of the way that Ash talks about Pikachu,

00:07:41.169 --> 00:07:45.060
We've actually seen them speak directly to each other before,

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:47.198
in the 20th Pokémon movie,

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:49.060
(yeah, they made twenty of these things)

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:50.258
"Pokémon: I Choose You!".

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.860
We actually learn an important fact about how humans and Pokémon,

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:58.640
and more specifically, how Ash and Pikachu are capable of understanding each other.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:03.638
In the movie's big climax, we actually hear a Pokémon talk:

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:08.520
"It's because I always want to be with you."

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:11.060
Now, to a lot of people, this seemed totally out of place,

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:14.520
and not just because Pikachu sounds like a rejected character from Rugrats.

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:18.300
No, it was because Pikachu just doesn't talk.

00:08:18.300 --> 00:08:20.060
It's not like Team Rocket's Meowth,

00:08:20.079 --> 00:08:23.138
"No matter what trash you throw at me, love wins in the end!"

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:28.460
But here, it doesn't seem like Pikachu is talking in the same way that Meowth does.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:34.120
As in, if you held up a microphone to Pikachu during this moment, it would probably sound like a very sad "Pikapika!".

00:08:34.120 --> 00:08:38.340
It's exactly the same narrative device that Detective Pikachu is using,

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:43.520
we're seeing things not as they literally are, but from the perspective of Ash,

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.920
who has the ability to understand what Pikachu is saying to him in this moment of desperation,

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:53.940
in the same way that Tim is somehow able to understand what Detective Pikachu is specifically saying to him.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.340
Now granted, this particular movie follows a different continuity,

00:08:57.340 --> 00:09:01.020
but the evidence of Ash and Pikachu understanding each other

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.660
on a deeper level than the outside world is definitely there.

00:09:04.900 --> 00:09:08.140
So if Pikachu were ever to be separated from Ash,

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:11.019
the human that he's closer to than anyone else in the world,

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.860
he would probably be in a pretty bad place.

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.160
Spending years travelling with a human who understood you well enough that you could have conversations with them,

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:24.300
only to then be thrust into a world full of humans that look at you and only hear the words "Pika pika!",

00:09:24.300 --> 00:09:29.940
would get pretty darn lonely, just like we see happen to Detective Pikachu in the trailer.

00:09:29.940 --> 00:09:31.690
"Oh my god, you can understand me?!"

00:09:31.690 --> 00:09:32.190
"Stop!"

00:09:32.190 --> 00:09:33.440
"I have been so lonely!".

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:35.980
So let's review our corkboard of evidence one last time,

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.660
We know that Detective Pikachu's location of Ryme City exists in the same world,

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:44.360
with known locations in the Pokémon universe, like Johto and Sinnoh.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:47.760
More specifically, from the presence of this Jigglypuff,

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.860
there's a much greater likelihood that this movie takes place in the same universe

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:55.120
as the one where Jigglypuff constantly is trolling Ash and his friends.

00:09:55.370 --> 00:10:00.100
We know that Detective Pikachu, just like Ash's Pikachu, loves caffeine,

00:10:00.100 --> 00:10:03.960
and has experience dealing with both Mr. Mimes and Charizards.

00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:12.120
But most importantly of all, we know that both of these Pokémon are capable of being understood by specific humans in specific contexts.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:20.620
So saying that Detective Pikachu is Ash's Pikachu might at first seem like a huge conclusion to draw from just a few minutes of trailer footage,

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.740
But this trailer is packed with so many clues and hints,

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:28.640
that all can be pointing towards a connection between "Detective Pikachu" and the original TV show.

00:10:28.640 --> 00:10:31.140
Sure it all may seem like circumstantial evidence,

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.879
but none of these details were placed in here by accident,

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:38.860
in fact, one of the most impressive things about this trailer is how much detail they do manage to pack in.

00:10:38.860 --> 00:10:44.060
So is Detective Pikachu an older, more grizzled version of Ash's Pikachu?

00:10:44.060 --> 00:10:45.910
Did we crack the case here?

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:47.940
Well, we'll just have to wait and see.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.500
But for now, the corkboard makes it a pretty compelling possibility.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:58.200
But hey, that's just a theory - a Film Theory!

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:01.740
Aaaand cut!

